The '''May Revolution (Guajicutea)''' was a political crisis in 2000 that followed the death of [[Julián Mera]] and the breakdown of the ruling [[Partido Unidad y Prosperidad|PUP]]'s succession arrangement. The crisis developed from an internal party dispute into a wider political confrontation after the publication of the Okami papers, the arrest and death of [[Raúl Makuán]], and financial pressure from the [[Adonian Community]]. It ended with the scheduling of elections for 8 July 2000 and the consolidation of [[Andrés Paredes]] as the leading figure of the restoration period.

== Background ==
By the late 1990s, [[Julián Mera]]'s administration was marked by declining health and increasing factional conflict. The PUP remained the dominant political organisation in Guajicutea, but disagreement over succession had become more visible in the years before Mera's death.

[[Emilio Mera]], Julián Mera's son, was supported by the party's hardline wing as the likely successor. [[Raúl Makuán]], a former head of the [[Organización de Servicios Especiales]] who had later entered the Assembly, broke with the PUP and formed the [[Partido Restauración]] in the days after the funeral.

== The Okami papers ==
Mera died on 14 April 2000. His state funeral drew a large public crowd and gave the succession dispute a prominent public setting. While foreign dignitaries were still in Guajicutea and security focused on maintaining order, Makuán announced the formation of the [[Partido Restauración]].

Around the same time, [[The Okami Times]] published leaked papers that became known as the Okami papers. Journalist [[Kenta Kawasaki (Journalist)|Kenta Kawasaki]] received the documents anonymously while in Guajicutea to report on Mera's funeral. The papers alleged that Emilio had ordered the death of a former lover through the OSE in 1999. The publication brought the succession dispute under public and international scrutiny.

== Arrests and pressure ==
Emilio ordered Makuán's arrest on 11 May on accusations that he had leaked false documents. Makuán died in custody on 12 May, and foul play was widely suspected. His death prompted protests and intensified the political crisis.

On 17 May, the [[Adonian Community]] froze Guajicutean foreign assets and paused loan disbursals tied to earlier development projects. The AC also requested Emilio's extradition and the presence of AC observers for future elections. [[Lucía Herrera]], the vice president and caretaker leader, placed Emilio under house arrest but did not pursue immediate extradition, since parts of the government believed that appeasing the AC would allow the crisis to settle and Emilio to be released later. By 19 May, protests had spread through the main urban centres.

== March and general strike ==

On 18 May, protesters marched on the presidential mansion. Security forces opened fire on the crowd, injuring three people. No deaths were reported. The protest was one of the first large demonstrations after Emilio's house arrest and helped widen the crisis beyond the party leadership.

The following day, hospitality workers began a general strike. The strike lasted one week and affected hotels, restaurants, and other tourism-linked services. It became the largest organised labour action of the crisis and increased pressure on the caretaker government to settle the dispute.

== Domestic unrest and press restrictions ==

Many Guajicuteans were aware that the [[Organización de Servicios Especiales]] had played a central role in public life, but few were willing to speak openly about it. After the announcement of Emilio's house arrest, student organisers from the [[University of Waribá]] were among the first to hold public demonstrations. The protests remained limited at first, in part because the media environment was still tightly controlled.

During the funeral procession, many foreign journalists were present in Guajicutea. The government revoked the visas of most of them and forcibly deported a number of reporters, although some remained in the country in hiding. Among them was [[Kenta Kawasaki (Journalist)|Kenta Kawasaki]], who had contact with the secret political action group [[Grupo Guajicutea Juventud]], founded in 1993 in Waribá and the northern jungles to coordinate local newspaper work and mutual aid.

== Election call and aftermath ==
On 25 May, Herrera announced that Emilio would be extradited to [[Areza]]. The government had already arranged his transfer, but the private jet carrying him suffered engine trouble and went missing over the Lacashe–Tapuya border before reaching its destination. Emilio remained wanted by the AC's justice authorities after the disappearance.

The crisis divided the PUP and created the opening used by [[Andrés Paredes]] to win the July election. The period that followed marked the transition from PUP dominance to the restoration-era political order.

